>> title: Birria de Res, Instant Pot
>> category: Stews & Braises
>> tags: beef, chiles, mexican, braise, instant pot, pressure cooker
>> counters: Instant Pot
>> dish: birria-de-res
>> kit: Instant Pot
>> aka: birria, birria tacos, taco de birria, quesabirria, red taco, instant pot birria, pressure cooker birria
>> pairs-with: corn-tortillas, salsa-roja
>> servings: 8
>> time: 1 hr 50 min
>> slack: forgiving — forty-five minutes or fifty is the same shreddable beef and the consomé only improves sitting; where it all goes is the four minutes toasting the chiles, because a chile taken to ash is bitter through the whole adobo and straining does not take it out
>> step.2: toast the chiles 4 min
>> step.3: blend, then strain
>> step.4: season the beef
>> step.5: 45 min high pressure, natural release 15 min
>> step.6: shred, skim the consomé

The liquid in the pot is the consomé, so this is the one variant that does not get less of it. Four cups go in and four cups are meant to come out.

Toast @dried guajillo chiles{6}(stemmed and seeded), @dried ancho chiles{3}(stemmed and seeded) dry on sauté in an #Instant Pot{} for ~toast{4%min}, until they smell of raisins and before they smell of ash.

Blend @&(~1)toasted chiles{} with @white onion{1%large}(quartered), @garlic{5%cloves}, @cider vinegar{3%Tbs}, @ground cumin{1%tsp}, @Mexican oregano{1%tsp}, @cinnamon stick{1}, @whole cloves{4}, @bay leaves{2}, @black peppercorns{1%tsp}, @water{4%cup}(950 mL; hot), then strain it through a #fine-mesh sieve{} — grit and skin left in the sieve are what make a consomé gritty.

Season @beef chuck{3%lb}(1.4 kg; cut in 3-in pieces), @beef short ribs{1%lb}(450 g) with @kosher salt{1%Tbs}.

Pressure cook @&(~1)seasoned beef{} under @&(~2)strained adobo{}, lid on and valve sealed: ~come to pressure{15%min}, then ~pressure cook{45%min} on high and ~natural release{15%min}. The oven version is judged by pulling at a piece; under a locked lid you cannot, which is what the forty-five minutes is standing in for.

Shred @&(~1)braised beef{} in the pot, then lift the meat out and skim the fat off the liquid underneath into a cup — that fat is what the tortillas are dipped in and the liquid under it is the consomé.
